    {
      "id": "nsu8kkm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Background: Rise of New Religions"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Vardhamana Mahavira was the 24th and last Tirthankara of Jainism, while Rishabhanatha was the first Tirthankara.",
        "The Three Jewels (Triratna) of Jainism consist of Right Faith (Samyak Darshana), Right Knowledge (Samyak Jnana), and Right Conduct (Samyak Charitra).",
        "Gautama Buddha delivered his first sermon at Sarnath, known as Dharmachakrapravartana, after attaining enlightenment at Bodh Gaya.",
        "Buddhism prescribes the Middle Path (Madhyama Pratipat) avoiding extreme indulgence and extreme asceticism.",
        "Major early council patrons include Ajatashatru (1st Council), Kalasoka (2nd Council), Ashoka (3rd Council), and Kanishka (4th Council)."
      ],
      "id": "za0dqo7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ct4w8vw",
      "content": "• By the 6th century BCE many people were dissatisfied with the complex rituals of Vedic religion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "iu22fgf",
      "content": "• The dominance of Brahmins and rigid caste system created social tensions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "21ycgor",
      "content": "• Expensive sacrifices (Yajnas) were difficult for common people."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fcsg7j2",
      "content": "• As a result, new religious movements like Jainism and Buddhism emerged."
    },
    {
      "id": "i8di7vl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Jainism"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7vrwrny",
      "content": "Jainism is one of the oldest religions in India. It emphasizes non‑violence, self‑discipline and spiritual purity."
    },
